\title{oddsrelay: Client for the 'OddsRelay' Sports Odds API}
\description{
Provides one function for each read endpoint of the 'OddsRelay' web service \url{https://oddsrelay.io/docs}, which serves bookmaker and exchange odds for the UK and Ireland. Requests ask for compressed replies, send entity tags so that unchanged data is not transferred again, wait as the service asks when it is busy, and signal refusals as classed conditions that carry the service's error code and request id. Replies are returned as lists and data frames, with the token and rate limit headers attached as attributes.
}
\section{Your key}{

Every function but \code{\link[=or_status]{or_status()}}, \code{\link[=or_coverage]{or_coverage()}}, \code{\link[=or_healthz]{or_healthz()}} and
\code{\link[=or_openapi]{or_openapi()}} needs a key. Each reads it from the \code{ODDSRELAY_KEY}
environment variable, or from its \code{.key} argument. Put the key in your
\code{.Renviron} rather than in a script. It travels only in the \code{Authorization}
header: never in a URL, and never in a printed request, a message or a
condition.
}

\section{What a call returns}{

The decoded JSON body, with every field the API sends, including fields it
adds after this release. Arrays of records become data frames. Each result
carries these attributes:
\itemize{
\item \code{tokens}: a list of \code{cost}, \code{used}, \code{remaining}, \code{limit} and \code{reset}, from
the \verb{X-Tokens-*} headers. \code{remaining} and \code{limit} may be \code{"unlimited"}.
\item \code{rate_limit}: a list of \code{limit}, \code{remaining} and \code{reset}, from the
\verb{X-RateLimit-*} headers.
\item \code{etag} and \code{request_id}.
\item \code{not_modified}: \code{TRUE} when the API answered 304 (see below).
}

A header the reply did not carry reads as \code{NA} (or \code{NULL} for \code{etag} and
\code{request_id}). A result that is not a data frame has the class
\code{oddsrelay_result}, which prints the body and one line of tokens instead of
every attribute; \code{unclass()} gives the plain list or string.
}

\section{Polling}{

For each URL it has fetched in this session, the package keeps the reply's
\code{ETag} and body, and sends the tag back as \code{If-None-Match}. When nothing has
changed the API answers 304, which spends no tokens, and the call returns
the body you already had with \code{attr(x, "not_modified")} set to \code{TRUE}.
}

\section{Errors}{

A refusal signals a condition of class \code{oddsrelay_error}, with the classes
\verb{oddsrelay_<code>} (from the body's \code{error.code}, such as
\code{oddsrelay_insufficient_tokens}) and \verb{oddsrelay_http_<status>} in front of
it. Each carries \code{status}, \code{code}, \code{request_id}, \code{error} (the whole error
object), \code{tokens} and \code{rate_limit}. A 402 also carries \code{cost}, \code{remaining}
and \code{resets_at}. Branch on the class or \code{code}, never on the message. Quote
\code{request_id} when you contact us.

A 429 is retried for you: the package waits the \code{Retry-After} seconds and
tries again, three requests in all, with a message before each wait, then
signals \code{oddsrelay_http_429} with \code{retry_after}. Network failures are left as
'httr2' signals them.
}

